### Runner pools

_(Runner layout)_:
: <select name="RUNNER_MODE" id="crf-runner-mode" onchange="crfPoolMode()">
  <option value="single" <?=crfp_sel($cfg,'RUNNER_MODE','single','single')?>>Single fleet (legacy)</option>
  <option value="pools" <?=crfp_sel($cfg,'RUNNER_MODE','pools','single')?>>Runner pools</option>
  </select>

:crf_mode_plug:
> **Single fleet** preserves one shared count and label set. **Runner pools** reserve independently scaled capacity behind labels such as `ci-pool-python`.
>
> Pools are scheduling routes, not security boundaries. They share the host kernel, runner image, network policy, privileged Docker mode, and writable caches.
:end

_(Requested runner backend)_:
: <select name="POOL_BACKEND" id="crf-pool-backend">
  <option value="classic" <?=crfp_sel($cfg,'POOL_BACKEND','classic','classic')?>>Classic persistent runners</option>
  <option value="scaleset" <?=crfp_sel($cfg,'POOL_BACKEND','scaleset','classic')?>>GitHub scale sets (gated)</option>
  </select>

:crf_backend_plug:
> Apply saves requested intent only. It never changes the effective backend. Use the explicit migration controls on Fleet after the compatibility gate passes.
:end

